Historical Development of E-Commerce

E-commerce, also known as electronic commerce, refers to the buying and selling of goods and services through electronic networks, particularly the internet. Long before online shopping became popular, businesses used early electronic systems to exchange information, such as orders and invoices. These systems quietly helped establish the foundations for modern electronic business transactions.

Early Foundations: Electronic Data Interchange
During the 1960s and 1970s, businesses began using computers to process and exchange commercial information. One important technology was Electronic Data Interchange (EDI), which allowed businesses to exchange standardised documents electronically rather than relying on paper-based communication. This made transactions faster, more organised, and far less dependent on physical paperwork. Although these early systems were expensive and mainly used by larger organisations, they established important foundations for e-commerce.

The Introduction of Electronic Shopping
In 1979, British inventor Michael Aldrich developed an early electronic shopping system known as teleshopping. It connected a modified television to a computer through a telephone line, allowing customers to access shopping information and place orders remotely. This was a major step because it demonstrated that shopping could take place without customers visiting a physical store. However, the technology was costly and never became widely available to the general public.

